 here  and then i'll be in a fairly  decent position yes the pawn here on h7  is now being attacked by the queen here  you know you could play pawn to g6 and  really stop a lot of that attack  but laska had different ideas instead of  after knight takes here on h5 instead of  taking with the queen here on h5  he played the move bishop takes on h7  more forceful approach to this  and then his opponent took here on h7  doesn't really do too much if he just  comes over here to h8 that's going to be  a losing proposition because now just  the queen takes here on h5  which you can see this is not going to  go well after  something like bishop here to g6 doesn't  really matter where black goes it's  going to end in a checkmate bb bishop  here to g5  bishop here g6 check  bishop here h6 but after the queen takes  this pawn is pinned down by this bishop  here uh so this is actually not going to  work and then after the king comes to g8  then queen here to h7 or  queen here 2 g7 both of those are going  to be checkmate so that does not work so  in the game this opponent correctly took  with his king here on  h7 instead of moving there to h8 but you  can start to see all the threats queen  here to h5 and now this opens up for the  rook to be able to get involved into the  game and attack the king here after the  king comes back here to  g8 now an additional sacrifice and that  is bishop takes here on  g7 and this is really  setting up for the threat of the queen  comes here to h8 because you if you look  at this same way i wouldn't take that  bishop i'll just go ahead and leave that  here it can't do that that will end in a  checkmate right away so does need to go  ahead and take this bishop here on g7  but after they do  queen 2 g4  king here 2 h7 uh it's not going to work  if they play king 2 h8 because rook here  2 f3 threatening swinging the rook over  here to h3 and they can try to stop it  with pawn two e5 to open up for the  queen to get involved to protect uh but  then after rook two h3 it's really just  delaying the inevitable after the queen  comes over here to h6 i can just go  ahead and take this and this is  checkmate but you can see how it's  different if the king is here on h7 and  that's why  black in the game played that king to  h7 so in the game  instead of the king 2 h8 we do see that  king 2 h7  white's going to continue same game plan  because the queen is going to fall so  rook 2 f3  pawn to e5 rook over here to h3 check  and then queen 2 h6 we do see the  exchange right here and then the move  queen to d7 in this spot black did  resign in the game because they're just  down too much it doesn't really matter  you can see the queen is attacking both  of these bishops  play could continue maybe rook over here  to e8 uh saying yes i know i'm gonna  lose one of these bishops here let's go  ahead and centralize my rooks  the bishop falls here maybe there's an  exchange on  this f4 square  the the queen can just start to gobble  up material you can see  white is up a lot of pawns compared to  black but also a queen compared to a  bishop in a rook  is definitely going to  win  just so much more horsepower in a queen  that they can use especially if you have  all these additional materials with the  ponds and the rook uh and with an  exposed king as well just makes it much  easier for white to dominate this game  so black did resign in this position  now that was the full lasker combination  but i want these videos to be extremely  instructional so  even if your opponent does not play all  the moves that you'll see you have a  good idea of how you may continue  so in this example the night taking here  on h5 it seems pretty reasonable but  it's actually a  bad move so they could try something  else one of the things that we talked  about is the bishop coming here to h7  and they can't really take here on h7  because we saw that was catastrophic but  they also can't come to h8 that would  end in checkmate pretty quickly so they  could try something like rook to  d8 wasn't played in the game but just  kind of showing you what could happen  and from here uh white still has some  really good options can take the knight  here after the bishop takes now we have  that in between move no reason to go  ahead and sacrifice yet because  we already know that if the king takes  here the queen's gonna  jump into the action right away and  check so instead the king kind of the  whole reason they played that knight  here to d8 is to move the king here to  f8 and now white can uh play the move  they could take that bishop if they want  to but i really like just clogging up  the center here with pawn to d4  and all of a sudden you can see whites  up a pawn and material with that h7  sacrifice or that h7 with the bishop  taking with the pawn sacrificing the  bishop that you know black cannot take  here things go south pretty quickly uh  both sides have their knights off the  board here and black can't castle is  king is  not in a safe spot so white can easily  get more material to attack his opponent  and white should have the upper hand  getting into the middle and the end game  so  even if your opponent doesn't take  exactly how you want to if you have one  side of the board that's overwhelmed  with material for in your advantage you  should definitely be looking at taking  advantage and trying to  take material on h7 take material on g7  get your queen and rook involved into  the game and really put a lot of  pressure on your opponent so  hopefully you guys enjoyed this video  looking at  a long time legend world champion alaska  in his deadly combination that he used  hopefully you guys can use some of this  knowledge in your own game and can start  to find some ways that you can win on  the kingside attack so thank you guys so  much for watching the video and i'll see  you guys in the next one

